WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.299 --> 00:00:01.400
Hi everyone, this is Jerry.

00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:04.510
And this is a game analysis between Donald
Byrne and Bobby Fischer.

00:00:04.510 --> 00:00:09.390
It was played in New York City on October
17th, 1956.

00:00:09.390 --> 00:00:11.250
At the time Fischer was thirteen years old.

00:00:11.250 --> 00:00:16.180
And soon after this game it was given the
title "Game of the Century".

00:00:16.180 --> 00:00:18.300
Rating and commenting are appreciated.

00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:21.060
And any questions you have for me feel free
to send one my way.

00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:22.660
Hope you enjoy the game.

00:00:22.660 --> 00:00:26.199
Byrne opens the game with the move knight
to f3, development.

00:00:26.199 --> 00:00:28.409
Fischer opens up with knight to f6, development.

00:00:28.409 --> 00:00:33.570
Pawn to c4, a move directed at controlling
a central square, d5.

00:00:33.570 --> 00:00:39.129
Pawn to g6, making way to fianchetto the king
bishop to g7.

00:00:39.129 --> 00:00:41.309
Knight to c3, development.

00:00:41.309 --> 00:00:43.579
Bishop to g7, development and making way to
castle.

00:00:43.579 --> 00:00:49.769
Pawn to d4, establishing a pawn in the center,
and making way for the queen bishop to develop.

00:00:49.769 --> 00:00:53.879
Castle, a developing move getting the king
safe.

00:00:53.879 --> 00:00:56.230
Bishop f4, development.

00:00:56.230 --> 00:01:00.769
Pawn to d5 signaling the Grunfeld Defense,
placing a pawn in the center and also striking

00:01:00.769 --> 00:01:02.640
at c4.

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:05.410
Queen to b3, indirectly attacking d5.

00:01:05.410 --> 00:01:10.790
Pawn captures pawn, queen captures pawn, and
now both the queen and bishop are directed

00:01:10.790 --> 00:01:14.000
at c7.

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:16.100
Fischer follows up with pawn to c6.

00:01:16.100 --> 00:01:21.600
If we just take a look back, it's worth noting
that if Fischer were to play pawn to c6 now,

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:26.090
white could continue pawn to e3, and now if
pawn takes pawn, the bishop has the option

00:01:26.090 --> 00:01:31.660
of taking on c4 developing a new piece, and
then getting ready to castle.

00:01:31.660 --> 00:01:38.510
However, by capturing on c4 right now in this
position black does not give white the possibility

00:01:38.510 --> 00:01:42.830
of taking on c4 with the bishop because the
pawn is still on e2.

00:01:42.830 --> 00:01:47.720
So as is often the case in chess, move order
matters, and this position right here is no

00:01:47.720 --> 00:01:49.610
exception.

00:01:49.610 --> 00:01:51.890
So after queen captures pawn... how to meet
this threat?

00:01:51.890 --> 00:01:57.020
You might be thinking, well why not develop
a piece and prevent this capture from occurring

00:01:57.020 --> 00:01:58.020
on c7?

00:01:58.020 --> 00:02:01.140
Well that might run into trouble with a move
like pawn to d5.

00:02:01.140 --> 00:02:06.470
The knight is shooed away from c6, and then
the pawn can be taken on c7.

00:02:06.470 --> 00:02:08.810
So that's why we see pawn to c6 here.

00:02:08.810 --> 00:02:14.230
Pawn to e4 is the continuation establishing
yet another pawn in the center, and making

00:02:14.230 --> 00:02:17.340
way for the king bishop's development.

00:02:17.340 --> 00:02:22.620
Knight to d7, getting ready to come to b6
where it would be attacking the queen.

00:02:22.620 --> 00:02:26.270
It temporarily obstructs the queen bishop's
development.

00:02:26.270 --> 00:02:30.750
An alternative would be knight to a6, staying
out of the bishop's way, and preparing a pawn

00:02:30.750 --> 00:02:35.340
to c5 advance where that pawn would strike
at d4.

00:02:35.340 --> 00:02:37.330
But instead we see the knight coming to d7.

00:02:37.330 --> 00:02:40.910
And now rook to d1, placing the rook opposite
the queen.

00:02:40.910 --> 00:02:42.140
Black has to be a bit careful.

00:02:42.140 --> 00:02:49.560
Whenever a rook is opposite your own queen,
you have to be careful of tactics.

00:02:49.560 --> 00:02:53.150
Knight to b6, attacking the queen and allowing
the bishop to come out.

00:02:53.150 --> 00:02:55.190
Queen to c5, a bit awkward.

00:02:55.190 --> 00:02:56.730
Maybe better is queen to b3.

00:02:56.730 --> 00:03:00.520
Although maybe Byrne was turned off to this
move as a result of a developing moved to

00:03:00.520 --> 00:03:03.930
e6 where the bishop is now attacking the queen.

00:03:03.930 --> 00:03:08.650
Fischer is able to take advantage of this
queen's placement shortly.

00:03:08.650 --> 00:03:12.310
Fischer follows up with bishop to g4, pinning
the knight to the rook.

00:03:12.310 --> 00:03:18.680
And now Byrne plays bishop to g5, violating
one of the well-known principles in chess

00:03:18.680 --> 00:03:21.980
which states: "you should not move the same
piece more than once in the opening".

00:03:21.980 --> 00:03:25.920
And this bishop initially came to f4, and
now it's going to g5.

00:03:25.920 --> 00:03:29.440
This is not to be recommended especially in
this position.

00:03:29.440 --> 00:03:35.340
Fischer is now able to execute a series of
exceptional moves, and it starts with knight

00:03:35.340 --> 00:03:42.080
to a4 deflecting the c3 knight from the defense
of the e4 pawn.

00:03:42.080 --> 00:03:44.840
The move that was played in the actual game
was queen to a3.

00:03:44.840 --> 00:03:50.180
However it's worth taking a look at what would
occur if knight takes knight.

00:03:50.180 --> 00:03:53.959
This is what would happen: knight takes pawn,
forking the queen and bishop.

00:03:53.959 --> 00:03:58.819
If queen takes pawn, that will result in queen
takes queen, bishop takes queen, rook to e8...

00:03:58.819 --> 00:04:02.430
after the bishop moves, the knight can come
here, discovered check on the king and the

00:04:02.430 --> 00:04:06.410
knight is going to be picked off, or maybe
even the rook.

00:04:06.410 --> 00:04:11.599
An alternative is bishop takes pawn... that
will result in knight takes queen, bishop

00:04:11.599 --> 00:04:15.690
takes queen, knight takes knight, black is
doing better in this position as well getting

00:04:15.690 --> 00:04:18.289
ready to take the bishop...pawn.

00:04:18.289 --> 00:04:23.840
Another choice after knight takes pawn is
queen to c1.

00:04:23.840 --> 00:04:29.379
That'll result in queen to a5, checking the
king, hitting the knight, hitting the bishop.

00:04:29.379 --> 00:04:33.949
If knight blocks, bishop takes knight, pawn
takes bishop, knight takes bishop.

00:04:33.949 --> 00:04:37.919
The queen can't take the knight because it's
protected.

00:04:37.919 --> 00:04:42.409
And one other choice is to block with the
bishop will result in knight takes bishop,

00:04:42.409 --> 00:04:45.169
queen takes knight, queen takes knight.

00:04:45.169 --> 00:04:48.550
Black is doing better in this variation as
well.

00:04:48.550 --> 00:04:52.340
One more choice after knight takes here is
queen to b4.

00:04:52.340 --> 00:04:56.740
That's going to result in knight takes bishop,
knight takes knight, bishop takes rook, king

00:04:56.740 --> 00:04:58.360
takes bishop, bishop takes pawn.

00:04:58.360 --> 00:05:03.289
And there is a nasty discovered check that's
in the air after the bishop moves.

00:05:03.289 --> 00:05:07.249
Black is doing better here as well.

00:05:07.249 --> 00:05:10.770
So the move that actually did get played was
queen to a3.

00:05:10.770 --> 00:05:12.330
And now we see knight takes knight.

00:05:12.330 --> 00:05:17.439
If queen takes knight, just knight takes pawn
forking the queen and bishop.

00:05:17.439 --> 00:05:24.401
So pawn takes knight was played, and now knight
still takes on e4, and allows this fork, bishop

00:05:24.401 --> 00:05:25.919
captures pawn on e7.

00:05:25.919 --> 00:05:27.830
The rook and queen are attacked.

00:05:27.830 --> 00:05:30.339
Fischer follows up with queen to b6.

00:05:30.339 --> 00:05:33.240
If bishop takes rook, that'll result in bishop
take bishop.

00:05:33.240 --> 00:05:35.139
The queen is getting hit.

00:05:35.139 --> 00:05:37.939
If queen to b3, knight takes c3.

00:05:37.939 --> 00:05:42.819
If queen takes knight, the bishop is going
to win the queen because there's a pin.

00:05:42.819 --> 00:05:45.139
If queen takes queen, pawn takes queen.

00:05:45.139 --> 00:05:46.430
The rook is still hit.

00:05:46.430 --> 00:05:50.069
The rook and knight are both converging on
a2.

00:05:50.069 --> 00:05:55.150
And even though the queens are off the board,
the white king is still in trouble.

00:05:55.150 --> 00:05:57.330
If rook to a1, rook to e8.

00:05:57.330 --> 00:05:58.530
King here, knight check.

00:05:58.530 --> 00:06:03.460
The f2 pawn will fall, and the white king
is going to have a giant headache to deal

00:06:03.460 --> 00:06:08.240
with because these bishops are going to give
him problems.

00:06:08.240 --> 00:06:11.600
So in this position the bishop did not take
the rook.

00:06:11.600 --> 00:06:16.069
Instead Byrne chose to develop the bishop
to c4.

00:06:16.069 --> 00:06:18.180
And now we see knight takes c3.

00:06:18.180 --> 00:06:21.909
If queen takes knight it's just rook comes
here, pins the bishop and the bishop will

00:06:21.909 --> 00:06:24.330
eventually be won.

00:06:24.330 --> 00:06:29.909
And so the move that was played is bishop
to c5 attacking the queen.

00:06:29.909 --> 00:06:30.919
Rook on f to e8.

00:06:30.919 --> 00:06:34.369
It's important that it's this rook that comes
to e8 because there are continuations where

00:06:34.369 --> 00:06:41.449
this a8 rook will function well on the a-file,
as is the case in the actual game.

00:06:41.449 --> 00:06:42.449
King to f1.

00:06:42.449 --> 00:06:45.710
And now the move that caught everyones attention,
bishop to e6!!

00:06:45.710 --> 00:06:51.740
Ignoring the threat on your own queen and
instead attacking the bishop.

00:06:51.740 --> 00:06:55.349
One continuation from here is to take the
knight.

00:06:55.349 --> 00:07:00.089
If that did occur, queen captures bishop would
be played seeing how there's a pin against

00:07:00.089 --> 00:07:01.089
the queen.

00:07:01.089 --> 00:07:06.689
If pawn takes queen, bishop takes queen.

00:07:06.689 --> 00:07:10.210
From this position, if bishop takes bishop
that'll lead to a checkmate in six.

00:07:10.210 --> 00:07:12.490
If you'd like to, pause the video and see
if you can figure it out.

00:07:12.490 --> 00:07:14.970
It's a checkmate in six problem.

00:07:14.970 --> 00:07:19.349
Okay, the solution starts out with queen to
b5 check.

00:07:19.349 --> 00:07:24.409
Bishop blocks, queen captures bishop, king
to g1, knight to e2 check, king to f1, knight

00:07:24.409 --> 00:07:29.319
to g3 that's double check...in double check
the king must always move, king to g1, queen

00:07:29.319 --> 00:07:35.439
to f1 check, rook captures queen the only
move, and now knight to e2 and that's mate...smothered

00:07:35.439 --> 00:07:37.819
mate.

00:07:37.819 --> 00:07:42.280
So the only other choice from here is to just
take the queen, and that's what Byrne played

00:07:42.280 --> 00:07:43.750
in the actual game.

00:07:43.750 --> 00:07:47.409
And that ends up putting white in a lot of
hot water.

00:07:47.409 --> 00:07:49.740
Bishop captures bishop checking the king.

00:07:49.740 --> 00:07:55.509
King to g1, knight e2, king f1, knight captures
pawn discovered check.

00:07:55.509 --> 00:08:00.659
If the rook blocks the check, that'll result
in pawn takes bishop.

00:08:00.659 --> 00:08:07.159
The only way to now move the queen from the
rook's attack is to play to c3, and defend

00:08:07.159 --> 00:08:09.440
the rook.

00:08:09.440 --> 00:08:14.569
That'll result in knight captures knight,
the bishop is hitting the queen.

00:08:14.569 --> 00:08:19.830
After queen captures bishop, rook to e1 and
that's mate.

00:08:19.830 --> 00:08:21.610
So that's why rook here is no good.

00:08:21.610 --> 00:08:24.880
So white unfortunately has to go back to g1.

00:08:24.880 --> 00:08:30.449
Knight gives another check, another check
discovered, and the knight is hitting the

00:08:30.449 --> 00:08:31.599
rook.

00:08:31.599 --> 00:08:38.649
King back to g1, and now pawn captures bishop,
the queen is being attacked by the rook.

00:08:38.649 --> 00:08:41.180
Queen to b4 attacking the bishop.

00:08:41.180 --> 00:08:47.860
Black first meets that threat with rook to
a4, attacking the queen, and defending the

00:08:47.860 --> 00:08:49.190
bishop.

00:08:49.190 --> 00:08:52.819
And after queen captures pawn, only now does
black take the rook.

00:08:52.819 --> 00:08:56.529
And now it's just a matter of winning the
game from this point on.

00:08:56.529 --> 00:09:00.061
Pawn to h2 getting ready to sneak the king
out and then get the rook involved, but it's

00:09:00.061 --> 00:09:02.300
too little too late.

00:09:02.300 --> 00:09:08.089
Rook captures pawn, king to h2, knight captures
pawn attacking the rook.

00:09:08.089 --> 00:09:11.529
Rook to e1, rook captures rook, and white
throws a check in first so that the bishop

00:09:11.529 --> 00:09:15.000
has to work himself into a pin, bishop f8.

00:09:15.000 --> 00:09:20.240
And knight captures rook, and now we see bishop
to d5.

00:09:20.240 --> 00:09:24.000
From this point on, black just organizes all
of his pieces and gets it to a point where

00:09:24.000 --> 00:09:25.399
they are coordinated.

00:09:25.399 --> 00:09:35.019
And as a result the white queen will not be
able to attack anything of black's.

00:09:35.019 --> 00:09:43.960
So knight to f3, knight to e4, queen to b8
attacking b7, pawn to b5.

00:09:43.960 --> 00:09:49.290
Pawn to h4 getting ready to come to h5 and
maybe smash up the kingside structure.

00:09:49.290 --> 00:09:53.579
But Fischer just stops that right away, pawn
to h5.

00:09:53.579 --> 00:09:58.230
Knight to e5 getting ready to come to d7 where
it would be attacking the pinned piece.

00:09:58.230 --> 00:10:00.930
So Fischer just gets out of the pin, king
to g7.

00:10:00.930 --> 00:10:06.280
And if you just take into account this position
right here, everything is protected of black's.

00:10:06.280 --> 00:10:10.220
The king guards the pawn, the bishop, these
pawns are protected, these pawns are protected,

00:10:10.220 --> 00:10:18.430
and this bishop here guards both the f7 pawn,
the c6 pawn, the knight, and the rook.

00:10:18.430 --> 00:10:23.060
Everything is strung together and as a result
the white queen can do nothing.

00:10:23.060 --> 00:10:26.180
King to g1, and now it's just a matter of
mating the white king.

00:10:26.180 --> 00:10:36.089
And it turns out in the following way: bishop
to c5 check, king to f1, knight g3 check,

00:10:36.089 --> 00:10:42.071
king to e1...these are all only moves, bishop
check, king to d1, bishop check, king to c1...the

00:10:42.071 --> 00:10:48.459
king is getting walked all the way along the
first rank, knight check, king to b1, knight

00:10:48.459 --> 00:10:52.790
c3 check, king to c1, rook to c2 and that's
mate.

00:10:52.790 --> 00:10:53.790
So that's all for this video.

00:10:53.790 --> 00:10:55.090
Hope you got something out of it.

00:10:55.090 --> 00:10:55.889
Take care, bye.

